1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,304
What if the internet you use every

2
00:00:03,304 --> 00:00:04,889
day wasn't in the sky,

3
00:00:04,889 --> 00:00:07,906
but at the bottom of the ocean?

4
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:09,430
It is.

5
00:00:09,530 --> 00:00:12,589
According to the United Nations' telecom

6
00:00:12,589 --> 00:00:13,507
agency,

7
00:00:13,507 --> 00:00:17,084
more than 99 percent of international data

8
00:00:17,084 --> 00:00:20,332
traffic travels through about 500 cables

9
00:00:20,332 --> 00:00:21,721
lying on the seabed,

10
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:24,239
stretching more than 1.7 million

11
00:00:24,239 --> 00:00:25,780
kilometers.

12
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:27,484
Every video call,

13
00:00:27,484 --> 00:00:28,928
every bank transfer,

14
00:00:28,928 --> 00:00:31,830
every message to another country.

15
00:00:41,283 --> 00:00:43,703
So how does the internet actually cross

16
00:00:43,703 --> 00:00:44,963
an ocean?

17
00:00:44,993 --> 00:00:46,368
Most people think it goes through

18
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:47,663
satellites.

19
00:00:47,663 --> 00:00:49,515
It mostly doesn't.

20
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:52,362
Satellites carry only a very small share

21
00:00:52,362 --> 00:00:54,490
of international traffic.

22
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:56,081
In the United States,

23
00:00:56,081 --> 00:00:58,498
government statistics put satellites at

24
00:00:58,498 --> 00:01:01,177
just 0.37 percent of the country's

25
00:01:01,177 --> 00:01:03,590
international capacity.

26
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:06,739
The cable research firm TeleGeography has

27
00:01:06,739 --> 00:01:09,186
checked the famous ninety nine percent

28
00:01:09,186 --> 00:01:11,388
claim for traffic between continents,

29
00:01:11,388 --> 00:01:12,477
and says it holds,

30
00:01:12,477 --> 00:01:15,969
even if nobody can measure it exactly.

31
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:18,817
Let me draw how it works.

32
00:01:18,837 --> 00:01:21,429
Your data leaves a data center and

33
00:01:21,429 --> 00:01:22,583
reaches the coast,

34
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:24,597
at a landing station.

35
00:01:24,827 --> 00:01:26,732
From there it enters a cable on

36
00:01:26,732 --> 00:01:27,967
the seabed.

37
00:01:28,257 --> 00:01:28,918
Inside,

38
00:01:28,918 --> 00:01:31,196
the data travels as pulses of light

39
00:01:31,196 --> 00:01:32,313
through glass fibers,

40
00:01:32,313 --> 00:01:35,252
and comes out at another landing station,

41
00:01:35,252 --> 00:01:37,447
on another continent.

42
00:01:37,717 --> 00:01:40,967
Then it goes on to its destination.

43
00:01:41,217 --> 00:01:42,577
Why light?

44
00:01:42,627 --> 00:01:44,513
Because light in glass can carry an

45
00:01:44,513 --> 00:01:47,238
enormous amount of data over very long

46
00:01:47,238 --> 00:01:48,467
distances.

47
00:01:48,607 --> 00:01:50,490
Each fiber is roughly as thin as

48
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:51,917
a human hair.

49
00:01:51,917 --> 00:01:53,993
Switch the light on and off extremely

50
00:01:53,993 --> 00:01:54,388
fast,

51
00:01:54,388 --> 00:01:56,672
and every flash becomes part of a

52
00:01:56,672 --> 00:01:57,687
message.

53
00:01:57,947 --> 00:02:00,201
Put many fibers in one cable,

54
00:02:00,201 --> 00:02:02,478
and one cable can carry the traffic

55
00:02:02,478 --> 00:02:04,216
of whole countries.

56
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:06,673
For most of its journey across the

57
00:02:06,673 --> 00:02:07,389
ocean,

58
00:02:07,389 --> 00:02:09,612
a cable is about as wide as

59
00:02:09,612 --> 00:02:11,200
a garden hose.

60
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:13,753
The thin glass fibers are wrapped in

61
00:02:13,753 --> 00:02:15,061
layers of plastic,

62
00:02:15,061 --> 00:02:17,530
and sometimes steel wire.

63
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:18,493
Near the shore,

64
00:02:18,493 --> 00:02:20,577
where fishing boats and anchors are,

65
00:02:20,577 --> 00:02:23,050
the cable gets extra armor.

66
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:24,955
And it is built to last at

67
00:02:24,955 --> 00:02:26,814
least 25 years.

68
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:30,033
So something this important must belong to

69
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:31,130
someone.

70
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:32,497
But who?

71
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:38,702
Who owns the cables at the bottom

72
00:02:38,702 --> 00:02:40,080
of the ocean?

73
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:41,046
It matters,

74
00:02:41,046 --> 00:02:43,715
because whoever owns the road decides who

75
00:02:43,715 --> 00:02:44,661
can use it,

76
00:02:44,661 --> 00:02:46,474
and how much it costs.

77
00:02:46,717 --> 00:02:49,227
Let me draw the old way.

78
00:02:49,417 --> 00:02:50,414
For decades,

79
00:02:50,414 --> 00:02:53,457
cables were owned by telephone companies.

80
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:55,460
Several of them would form a club,

81
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:57,207
called a consortium.

82
00:02:57,237 --> 00:02:58,833
Each member paid for a share of

83
00:02:58,833 --> 00:02:59,612
the cable,

84
00:02:59,612 --> 00:03:02,207
and got a share of its capacity.

85
00:03:02,217 --> 00:03:02,633
Then,

86
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:04,801
in the late nineteen nineties,

87
00:03:04,801 --> 00:03:06,991
new companies started building private

88
00:03:06,991 --> 00:03:07,626
cables,

89
00:03:07,626 --> 00:03:10,254
and selling the capacity to anyone who

90
00:03:10,254 --> 00:03:11,511
wanted it.

91
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:14,460
Then the users changed.

92
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:16,646
In 2016,

93
00:03:16,646 --> 00:03:18,753
internet backbone companies,

94
00:03:18,753 --> 00:03:20,885
the carriers that move traffic between

95
00:03:20,885 --> 00:03:21,571
networks,

96
00:03:21,571 --> 00:03:24,176
still used most of the international

97
00:03:24,176 --> 00:03:25,250
bandwidth.

98
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:27,146
By twenty twenty-four,

99
00:03:27,146 --> 00:03:29,706
content and cloud companies used almost

100
00:03:29,706 --> 00:03:31,470
three quarters of it.

101
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,001
That means a handful of names:

102
00:03:34,001 --> 00:03:34,776
Google,

103
00:03:34,776 --> 00:03:35,552
Meta,

104
00:03:35,552 --> 00:03:38,024
Microsoft and Amazon.

105
00:03:38,283 --> 00:03:40,923
And demand keeps exploding.

106
00:03:40,923 --> 00:03:43,106
Between twenty twenty and twenty

107
00:03:43,106 --> 00:03:44,053
twenty-four,

108
00:03:44,053 --> 00:03:46,939
international bandwidth demand tripled,

109
00:03:46,939 --> 00:03:50,863
to more than 6.4 petabits per second.

110
00:03:51,043 --> 00:03:51,782
Streaming,

111
00:03:51,782 --> 00:03:54,328
cloud computing and now artificial

112
00:03:54,328 --> 00:03:57,384
intelligence all need more capacity than

113
00:03:57,384 --> 00:03:58,494
ever.

114
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:00,280
So the tech giants stopped renting and

115
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:01,300
started building.

116
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:02,886
One example:

117
00:04:02,886 --> 00:04:06,230
a cable called Two Africa.

118
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:08,753
Announced by Meta in twenty twenty with

119
00:04:08,753 --> 00:04:11,066
African and global telecom partners,

120
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:14,545
it was planned at 37,000 kilometers,

121
00:04:14,545 --> 00:04:16,664
almost the circumference of the Earth,

122
00:04:16,664 --> 00:04:19,211
to connect 23 countries in Africa,

123
00:04:19,211 --> 00:04:21,171
the Middle East and Europe.

124
00:04:21,417 --> 00:04:23,688
The companies that fill the cables with

125
00:04:23,688 --> 00:04:25,733
data are now the ones paying to

126
00:04:25,733 --> 00:04:26,787
lay them.

127
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:28,905
Owning the cable means they don't have

128
00:04:28,905 --> 00:04:29,530
to wait,

129
00:04:29,530 --> 00:04:30,800
or pay someone else,

130
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,477
for capacity.

131
00:04:32,727 --> 00:04:35,122
The internet's underwater roads are

132
00:04:35,122 --> 00:04:37,399
becoming private highways.

133
00:04:37,650 --> 00:04:40,850
But owning a cable is one thing.

134
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:41,752
Building one,

135
00:04:41,752 --> 00:04:43,788
and fixing it at the bottom of

136
00:04:43,788 --> 00:04:44,444
the sea,

137
00:04:44,444 --> 00:04:45,949
is another.

138
00:04:50,383 --> 00:04:52,668
So who builds these cables,

139
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:54,923
and who fixes them?

140
00:04:55,073 --> 00:04:57,675
Very few companies on Earth can do

141
00:04:57,675 --> 00:04:58,535
it.

142
00:04:58,783 --> 00:05:00,733
Let's open one up.

143
00:05:00,923 --> 00:05:02,019
At the center,

144
00:05:02,019 --> 00:05:04,633
the glass fibers that carry the light.

145
00:05:04,633 --> 00:05:05,501
Around them,

146
00:05:05,501 --> 00:05:07,803
layers of plastic that protect them.

147
00:05:07,803 --> 00:05:09,036
Sometimes,

148
00:05:09,036 --> 00:05:10,643
steel wire for strength.

149
00:05:10,653 --> 00:05:11,790
And near the coast,

150
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:14,341
extra armor against anchors and fishing

151
00:05:14,341 --> 00:05:15,173
gear.

152
00:05:15,283 --> 00:05:16,853
Simple in theory.

153
00:05:16,853 --> 00:05:18,663
Very hard to make in one piece

154
00:05:18,663 --> 00:05:20,451
across an entire ocean,

155
00:05:20,451 --> 00:05:23,167
so that it survives for decades under

156
00:05:23,167 --> 00:05:24,220
the sea.

157
00:05:24,483 --> 00:05:25,587
For decades,

158
00:05:25,587 --> 00:05:28,066
three companies have dominated building

159
00:05:28,066 --> 00:05:29,693
and laying these cables.

160
00:05:29,693 --> 00:05:32,226
SubCom from the United States,

161
00:05:32,226 --> 00:05:33,479
NEC from Japan,

162
00:05:33,479 --> 00:05:36,037
and Alcatel Submarine Networks from

163
00:05:36,037 --> 00:05:36,893
France.

164
00:05:37,183 --> 00:05:40,294
And one newcomer is growing faster than

165
00:05:40,294 --> 00:05:41,482
all of them:

166
00:05:41,482 --> 00:05:42,302
HMN Tech,

167
00:05:42,302 --> 00:05:43,750
from China.

168
00:05:44,017 --> 00:05:46,437
Then there are the repairs.

169
00:05:46,437 --> 00:05:47,811
When a cable breaks,

170
00:05:47,811 --> 00:05:50,607
a specialized cable repair ship has to

171
00:05:50,607 --> 00:05:51,748
sail to the spot,

172
00:05:51,748 --> 00:05:54,520
find the damaged section on the seabed,

173
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:56,384
bring it up and repair it at

174
00:05:56,384 --> 00:05:57,217
sea.

175
00:05:57,227 --> 00:05:59,238
These ships are stationed around the

176
00:05:59,238 --> 00:05:59,663
world,

177
00:05:59,663 --> 00:06:01,157
ready to go.

178
00:06:01,307 --> 00:06:03,554
When two cables were damaged in the

179
00:06:03,554 --> 00:06:06,452
Baltic Sea in November twenty twenty-four,

180
00:06:06,452 --> 00:06:09,209
both were repaired within 2 weeks.

181
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:12,240
The world's data runs through a handful

182
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:15,030
of factories and a small fleet of

183
00:06:15,030 --> 00:06:15,940
ships.

184
00:06:16,130 --> 00:06:18,737
If you control who builds the cable,

185
00:06:18,737 --> 00:06:20,844
you have a say in who connects

186
00:06:20,844 --> 00:06:21,971
to whom.

187
00:06:22,217 --> 00:06:24,757
And those repair ships are busy.

188
00:06:24,757 --> 00:06:27,221
Because cables break far more often than

189
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:28,561
you might think.

190
00:06:33,017 --> 00:06:36,017
So what happens when a cable breaks?

191
00:06:36,017 --> 00:06:37,578
One cut can slow down,

192
00:06:37,578 --> 00:06:38,610
or switch off,

193
00:06:38,610 --> 00:06:41,856
the internet of a whole region.

194
00:06:42,117 --> 00:06:44,605
According to the International Cable

195
00:06:44,605 --> 00:06:46,035
Protection Committee,

196
00:06:46,035 --> 00:06:49,269
there are 150 to 200 cable faults

197
00:06:49,269 --> 00:06:51,527
around the world every year.

198
00:06:51,697 --> 00:06:54,601
And 70 to 80 percent of them

199
00:06:54,601 --> 00:06:56,919
are accidents caused by people,

200
00:06:56,919 --> 00:06:59,627
mostly fishing gear and ships' anchors.

201
00:06:59,657 --> 00:07:01,097
Not sabotage.

202
00:07:01,097 --> 00:07:03,769
Just everyday activity at sea.

203
00:07:04,017 --> 00:07:06,307
In February twenty twenty-four,

204
00:07:06,307 --> 00:07:08,757
it happened in the Red Sea.

205
00:07:08,857 --> 00:07:11,287
A cargo ship called the Rubymar was

206
00:07:11,287 --> 00:07:13,716
hit by a missile attack and later

207
00:07:13,716 --> 00:07:14,537
sank.

208
00:07:14,767 --> 00:07:17,113
American officials said its dragging

209
00:07:17,113 --> 00:07:19,501
anchor most likely cut three cables:

210
00:07:19,501 --> 00:07:20,119
Seacom,

211
00:07:20,119 --> 00:07:21,164
A A E one,

212
00:07:21,164 --> 00:07:22,487
and E I G.

213
00:07:22,487 --> 00:07:25,362
One internet provider estimated these

214
00:07:25,362 --> 00:07:28,525
cables carried about 25 percent of the

215
00:07:28,525 --> 00:07:30,563
traffic in the region.

216
00:07:30,817 --> 00:07:32,637
Let me draw why that wasn't a

217
00:07:32,637 --> 00:07:34,017
disaster.

218
00:07:34,057 --> 00:07:35,661
The Red Sea has more than a

219
00:07:35,661 --> 00:07:37,107
dozen cables.

220
00:07:37,107 --> 00:07:38,318
When three go down,

221
00:07:38,318 --> 00:07:40,518
traffic is sent around the break,

222
00:07:40,518 --> 00:07:42,107
through the others.

223
00:07:42,177 --> 00:07:44,795
It gets slower and more expensive,

224
00:07:44,795 --> 00:07:46,717
but it keeps flowing.

225
00:07:47,137 --> 00:07:49,092
But what if a country has only

226
00:07:49,092 --> 00:07:50,344
one cable?

227
00:07:50,583 --> 00:07:53,523
That is what happened to Tonga.

228
00:07:53,583 --> 00:07:55,314
On January fifteenth,

229
00:07:55,314 --> 00:07:56,793
twenty twenty-two,

230
00:07:56,793 --> 00:08:00,403
a huge volcano erupted near its islands.

231
00:08:00,703 --> 00:08:03,655
The eruption tore 80 kilometers of its

232
00:08:03,655 --> 00:08:05,503
cable into pieces.

233
00:08:05,703 --> 00:08:07,930
Tonga's main link to the rest of

234
00:08:07,930 --> 00:08:09,643
the world was gone.

235
00:08:09,873 --> 00:08:11,318
It took more than five weeks to

236
00:08:11,318 --> 00:08:12,122
bring it back,

237
00:08:12,122 --> 00:08:15,653
and about 90 kilometers of new cable.

238
00:08:15,917 --> 00:08:18,598
The internet is only as strong as

239
00:08:18,598 --> 00:08:20,807
its number of routes.

240
00:08:21,217 --> 00:08:23,115
Where there are many cables,

241
00:08:23,115 --> 00:08:25,637
a cut is an inconvenience.

242
00:08:25,837 --> 00:08:27,135
Where there is one,

243
00:08:27,135 --> 00:08:29,060
it is a blackout.

244
00:08:29,317 --> 00:08:32,770
And if cables are this important,

245
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:35,908
governments want a say in where they

246
00:08:35,908 --> 00:08:36,857
go.

247
00:08:41,317 --> 00:08:43,797
Which is why governments are now fighting

248
00:08:43,797 --> 00:08:45,637
over the seabed.

249
00:08:45,707 --> 00:08:48,536
The cable that carries your data passes

250
00:08:48,536 --> 00:08:49,025
through,

251
00:08:49,025 --> 00:08:50,028
and lands in,

252
00:08:50,028 --> 00:08:51,657
other countries.

253
00:08:51,917 --> 00:08:54,587
Take a cable called SEA-ME-WE six,

254
00:08:54,587 --> 00:08:56,178
from Southeast Asia,

255
00:08:56,178 --> 00:08:57,769
through the Middle East,

256
00:08:57,769 --> 00:08:59,717
to Western Europe.

257
00:09:00,097 --> 00:09:01,486
In early twenty twenty,

258
00:09:01,486 --> 00:09:03,420
the Chinese company HMN Tech was chosen

259
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:04,267
to build it.

260
00:09:04,267 --> 00:09:08,000
Then the United States government pressed

261
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,467
the members to drop it.

262
00:09:10,687 --> 00:09:11,532
In the end,

263
00:09:11,532 --> 00:09:14,131
the American company SubCom won the

264
00:09:14,131 --> 00:09:14,760
contract,

265
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:16,493
with a bid that was about a

266
00:09:16,493 --> 00:09:17,771
third more expensive,

267
00:09:17,771 --> 00:09:19,887
according to Light Reading.

268
00:09:20,117 --> 00:09:21,516
Two Chinese carriers,

269
00:09:21,516 --> 00:09:24,338
China Telecom and China Mobile,

270
00:09:24,338 --> 00:09:25,886
left the project.

271
00:09:26,150 --> 00:09:29,010
It also happened across the Pacific.

272
00:09:29,090 --> 00:09:31,260
The Pacific Light Cable Network,

273
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:33,211
owned by Google and Meta,

274
00:09:33,211 --> 00:09:35,402
was meant to link the United States

275
00:09:35,402 --> 00:09:36,660
with Hong Kong.

276
00:09:37,010 --> 00:09:39,373
After an American security review

277
00:09:39,373 --> 00:09:41,173
recommended rejecting that leg,

278
00:09:41,173 --> 00:09:42,500
it was dropped.

279
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:45,136
The cable now carries data from the

280
00:09:45,136 --> 00:09:47,904
United States only to Taiwan and the

281
00:09:47,904 --> 00:09:49,426
Philippines.

282
00:09:49,683 --> 00:09:52,243
Let me draw what is happening.

283
00:09:52,333 --> 00:09:53,241
On one side,

284
00:09:53,241 --> 00:09:56,014
cables built and owned by American,

285
00:09:56,014 --> 00:09:58,873
Japanese and European companies.

286
00:09:58,873 --> 00:09:59,978
On the other,

287
00:09:59,978 --> 00:10:02,007
a growing network built by Chinese

288
00:10:02,007 --> 00:10:03,283
companies.

289
00:10:03,323 --> 00:10:05,483
Each country decides who may land a

290
00:10:05,483 --> 00:10:07,163
cable on its coast.

291
00:10:07,163 --> 00:10:07,886
Slowly,

292
00:10:07,886 --> 00:10:11,229
the seabed is splitting into two networks.

293
00:10:11,483 --> 00:10:13,773
And the stakes keep growing.

294
00:10:13,773 --> 00:10:16,832
TeleGeography now tracks more than 600

295
00:10:16,832 --> 00:10:18,983
active and planned cables.

296
00:10:19,123 --> 00:10:21,593
Every new one is a choice:

297
00:10:21,593 --> 00:10:22,640
who builds it,

298
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:23,955
who pays for it,

299
00:10:23,955 --> 00:10:25,629
and where it lands.

300
00:10:25,883 --> 00:10:29,283
The internet is not a cloud.

301
00:10:29,383 --> 00:10:30,263
It is glass,

302
00:10:30,263 --> 00:10:31,369
plastic and steel,

303
00:10:31,369 --> 00:10:32,927
lying on the ocean floor,

304
00:10:32,927 --> 00:10:34,710
built by a few companies,

305
00:10:34,710 --> 00:10:36,291
paid for more and more by the

306
00:10:36,291 --> 00:10:37,397
biggest tech firms,

307
00:10:37,397 --> 00:10:39,631
repaired by a small fleet of ships,

308
00:10:39,631 --> 00:10:42,163
and argued over by governments.

309
00:10:42,163 --> 00:10:45,059
Every time you send a message to

310
00:10:45,059 --> 00:10:46,767
another country,

311
00:10:46,767 --> 00:10:48,765
it takes this journey,

312
00:10:48,765 --> 00:10:50,474
as pulses of light,

313
00:10:50,474 --> 00:10:53,100
at the bottom of the sea.

314
00:10:53,350 --> 00:10:55,656
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

315
00:10:55,656 --> 00:10:58,407
we follow the money behind another hidden

316
00:10:58,407 --> 00:11:00,700
link of the world economy.

317
00:11:00,830 --> 00:11:03,299
This is The Hidden Economics.
